<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime.

  `codesign --options runtime` is not optional: Apple's notary service rejects
  anything without it. The hardened runtime then forbids, among other things,
  making memory both writable and executable -- which is precisely what a JIT
  does, and .NET is a JIT. An app signed with the hardened runtime and none of
  the keys below signs cleanly, notarizes cleanly, staples cleanly and then
  dies on launch, so these are load bearing rather than boilerplate.

  Each one is here for a reason and none should be added casually; every
  entitlement is a hole in the protection the hardened runtime is providing.
  Both of the ones below were confirmed necessary by signing a build without
  them and launching it: the process dies immediately with

      Failed to create CoreCLR, HRESULT: 0x80070008

  which is the JIT being refused executable memory, reported as though the
  machine were out of it.

  Deliberately absent:

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        Would let the process load libraries signed by somebody else. The
        obvious candidates are the native libraries beside the executable --
        Skia, HarfBuzz, the Avalonia macOS backend, SQLite -- which arrive
        from NuGet already signed by their own authors and are loaded with
        dlopen rather than linked. The release re-signs every one of them with
        the same Developer ID as the application, so library validation has
        nothing to object to, and a build that did need this key would be a
        build that had missed signing something.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
        Lets DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES and friends apply to the process, which is
        the classic route to injecting code into a signed application. .NET
        configures itself through DOTNET_* variables rather than DYLD_* ones,
        and this build is self-contained, so nothing here needs it.

    com.apple.security.app-sandbox
        Sandboxing is an App Store requirement, not a Developer ID one. The
        whole job of this application is to read a folder of films that the
        user chose anywhere on their disk, so a sandbox would have to be
        opened back up with security-scoped bookmarks before it did anything.
        Worth doing one day; not part of making the download open.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- The CoreCLR JIT maps executable pages at runtime. Without this the
       process is killed the first time it compiles a method, which is during
       startup, so the app never draws a window. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- .NET falls back to plain RWX pages when its write-xor-execute mode is
       unavailable, and parts of the runtime -- notably the interpreter paths
       and some diagnostics -- still allocate that way. Without this the
       failure is intermittent by workload rather than immediate, which is the
       worse kind. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
